A||||6||AkursedX||Powdercoating the rims.  Question.||||5||
Z||000000||AkursedX||08-02-2001||10:54 PM||||Ok, I am going to strip my 87GT rims.  My question is, can I powdercoat the entire rim?  What I mean is can I do the honeycomb part and the lip?  Or will the lip get scrtached too much?  But I think it would be more durable than clearcoat would be.  I just want to see if anyone else has done this.<P>What I plan on doing is painting my GT completely black, and then powdercoating my rims completely black, and my windows are tinted already.  I think it would look pretty cool.<P>Any feedack is appreciated  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||gt boy||08-02-2001||11:22 PM||||I must admit I love powder coating on things that don't have tools touching them...but as much as powder coating looks great it really can be a pain in the @ss.  I have had many motorcycles that I did much powder coating to and once you got a chip in it...it is impossible to fix properly(unless someone knows a way?)  I rather prefer painting things with a good quality paint(automotive)instead of powder coating because you can fix any flaws that you might get from stones,tools, etc.  once you get a chip in p/c it can very easily grow to a larger chip and then peel off altogether and if you get moisture under it(especially with salt water) things will start to corrode underneath leading to more headaches....and lets not mention striping the powder coating off to refinish the part...this has been  my experience anyway. <P>don't mean to put it down ...but in my eyes there is a place for powder coating and paint <p>[This message has been edited by gt boy (edited 08-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||08-02-2001||11:27 PM||||i like the idea anyway. i don't know anything about powder coating though. i want to do one of mine that way. probaly black, but i'm looking for inpiration for another color.||||reg||1
Z||000003||artherd||08-02-2001||11:53 PM||||Powerdercoating tip: Sandblast (or rough sand) the part first!!  Smooth surfaces are horrible (and flake off easially once chiped) If you rough it up properly first however, the coating should almost be bulletproof.<P>Personally, I would love to see what a CLEAR powerdercoat would look like on the striped aluminum rims.  Pretty nice I think. They're really neat and shiney under all that crud.<P>But yeah, rough them way up and you should be allright.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||08-03-2001||09:05 AM||||If you do powdercoat, don't forget to mast the wheel mounting surface. It should be bare metal.||||reg||1
